# {{ .inputs.repo_name }}

{{ if .inputs.description }}{{ .inputs.description }}{{ else }}A hollis-labs class 2 Substrate service.{{ end }}

Class 2 tool (project/1 class model, RC1): `project.yaml` is the descriptor, `deploy.yaml` is read by `fleet-install`.

## Build

```sh
go mod tidy
go run github.com/hollis-labs/substrate/cmd/substrate pins sync --app .
go mod tidy
go build -o {{ .inputs.repo_name }} ./cmd/{{ .inputs.repo_name }}
./{{ .inputs.repo_name }} serve
```

## Deploy

`fleet-install deploy {{ .inputs.repo_name }} --commit <sha> --dry-run`, then without `--dry-run`. Set the host alias in `deploy.yaml` first. Render with `target_os=darwin target_arch=arm64` for an Apple Silicon Mac (defaults: linux/amd64); Cerberus `supervisor: auto` selects the native supervisor. The installed PATH binary runs independently of the source checkout; keep operator config/credentials outside the repository.

## Run under Cerberus

Fleet-install provisions `settings.runtime.install_root` while placing the binary. The generated
Cerberus `config.dir` uses that stable directory, so startup needs no source checkout and survives
release pruning. Install before the first resource apply; keep app data/config in their XDG paths.
Cerberus only supervises the installed binary (`{{ .inputs.repo_name }}.cerberus.yaml`, no build, no artifact path):

```sh
cerberus validate {{ .inputs.repo_name }}.cerberus.yaml
cerberus register {{ .inputs.repo_name }}.cerberus.yaml
cerberus resource apply {{ .inputs.repo_name }} --ack
```

## Auth

Default `{{ .inputs.auth }}`. `enforce` needs a bearer token in `{{ upper (replace "-" "_" .inputs.repo_name) }}_TOKEN`. `{{ upper (replace "-" "_" .inputs.repo_name) }}_AUTH=loopback_open` requires `serve --dev` on a loopback bind; the service refuses to start otherwise. `/healthz` names the active mode. The generated Cerberus command uses `serve --managed`, which defaults to enforce and refuses an explicit loopback_open override or `--dev`, even on loopback.

The token never goes in a file of this repository. Under Cerberus an `enforce` service exits at start without it, so before `cerberus resource apply` put `{{ upper (replace "-" "_" .inputs.repo_name) }}_TOKEN` where the unit reads it: a Cerberus secret reference in the manifest's `config.env`, or an owner-only env file named by `config.env_file` (an absolute path outside this repository). The commented lines in `{{ .inputs.repo_name }}.cerberus.yaml` show where.

## After a deploy

`deploy.yaml` restarts through Cerberus (`restart: {via: cerberus, resource: {{ .inputs.repo_name }}}`). fleet-install runs that restart itself only when the step has `run: true` and the deploy is run by the local user on that host; otherwise it prints a request for a human to approve and the deploy stays pending until `--restart-done`.

Health 200 is not proof of the new build: the manifest must keep `command[0]` on the fleet-installed path, otherwise Cerberus can restart an older copy. After a deploy check that the running executable is the new version (on Linux, `readlink /proc/<pid>/exe`; on macOS, inspect the running executable with `lsof -p <pid>` and compare it with the fleet-installed binary). deploy.yaml has no `health.version_json`: this service's `/healthz` does not report a version, so the check would always fail; add the key once the service exposes one.

## Installed configuration

The binary reads `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/{{ .inputs.repo_name }}/config.yaml` when present (fallback
`~/.config/{{ .inputs.repo_name }}/config.yaml`). `SUBSTRATE_CONFIG` explicitly selects another file.
Config discovery creates nothing and does not read from the checkout. Store credentials in an
operator-owned environment file or secret provider outside the repository.

